{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:f0283aea50bda2f2c539625a00b5a9ca143be32428b78eb58224ebee0d51d933","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.95},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.15}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"RESOLVING SERIES AND THE TWO FORKS THAT MOVE THE ANSWER MORE THAN MACRO DOES. Construction: 10y nominal benchmark minus 10y inflation swap OR TIPS breakeven, for each of 7 countries, GDP-weighted, averaged over 60 months. FORK 1 - swap vs breakeven: because breakeven is DEFINED as nominal minus the linker yield, \"nominal minus TIPS breakeven\" just returns the TIPS real yield, inclusive of the TIPS liquidity premium (+10-25 bp vs the true ex-ante real rate). \"Nominal minus inflation swap\" returns something else entirely: USD 10y zero-coupon inflation swaps have run ~15-30 bp ABOVE CPI breakevens (swap vs cash basis, CPI floor and indexation-lag conventions), so the swap route yields a real rate ~15-30 bp LOWER. The question's \"/\" is therefore a ~20-40 bp fork on the P50 - larger than most plausible macro disagreement about the 2032-36 path. FORK 2 - weighting: \"GDP-weighted\" is unspecified while the lock says PPP. At PPP, G7 weights are approximately US 0.523, Japan 0.113, Germany 0.105, UK 0.075, France 0.075, Italy 0.062, Canada 0.046; at market exchange rates the US rises to ~0.57 and Japan falls to ~0.07, which lifts the aggregate by ~+0.15 pp because Japan is the low outlier. COVERAGE GAPS: Italy and Canada have thin 10y inflation-swap markets and no deep linker curve (BTP Italia is CPI-FOI-linked and short); Japan's 10y JPY inflation swap is barely transactable and the JGBi curve is sparse. For at least 3 of 7 countries the stated construction is not cleanly computable, so resolution will interpolate or substitute survey breakevens - a further ~15 bp of construction noise. Also: if any G7 central bank reinstitutes yield-curve control or explicit caps (the \"financial repression\" leg of CQ6-C/D), the nominal benchmark stops being a market price and the computed real yield becomes an administered number - a definitional break, not an observation.\nLEVELS. Anchor: US 10y TIPS real 2.60% (locked, FRED DFII10, 30 Oct 2026). For 2032-36 I take US 10y real averaging ~2.30%: upward pressure from gross public debt >100% of GDP (US ~142% by 2031), net interest 2% -> 3% of GDP, eroding sovereign convenience yields, levered basis-trade absorption of duration, retiree DISSAVING, and AI capital demand; downward pressure from the aging saving glut, a lower frictionless r*, and post-bust demand deficit. The locked IMF framing - (r - g) worsens by ~+1.0 pp even where r* eases - is exactly the \"g collapses faster than r\" shape, which keeps real yields high in level terms without requiring an r* surge.\nCountry reals used (2032-36 averages): US 2.30, Japan 0.50, Germany 1.10, UK 1.80, France 1.80, Italy 2.60, Canada 1.80. PPP-weighted = 1.203 + 0.057 + 0.116 + 0.135 + 0.135 + 0.161 + 0.083 = 1.89%. Round P50 to 1.95 to split the swap/breakeven and PPP/market-rate forks.\nTAILS, RIGHT-SKEWED. P10 0.90: CQ6-D demand shortfall plus explicit repression - QE/YCC return, breakevens hold up while nominals are capped, Japanification of Germany and Canada. P90 3.15: CQ6-C fiscal dominance with a term-premium spike; Debt-at-Risk P95 at 117% of GDP (+2.4 pp on a 20% AI-led equity shock) is the stated tail, and a sustained 5-year AVERAGE above 3% requires that tail to persist rather than spike, which is why P90 is 3.15 and not 4.0.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.469Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes the Structure of Earnings Survey 2026 wave (median gross hourly earnings by NACE section, reference year 2026) and the F+Q vs J+K+M median gap moves by less than 2 pp versus SES 2022 - or Eurostat/the OECD confirms no sectoral real median hourly wage aggregate will be constructed for OECD-38.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030 (SES 2026 microdata/standard tables release)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 falls from 12 to 5-7: it confirms both that the composition drag neutralizes the F+Q advantage and that the 2036 endpoint is unobservable. If instead the gap has already opened by >= 4 pp in four years, CQ1 rises to 20-24.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The OECD's published CPI-excluding-food-and-energy aggregate for the OECD total: whether the OECD continues to publish the all-38 aggregate including Turkey, or introduces/promotes an 'OECD excluding high-inflation members' or 'advanced-economy' core aggregate as the headline object.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If an ex-high-inflation core aggregate becomes the standard published series, CQ2 falls from 50 to ~32 and CQ6 shifts C 28 -> 17, D 11 -> 18, B 50 -> 54. If the all-38 aggregate remains the only published core, CQ2 rises to ~56 and C holds at 28-31. This is the largest single resolution-mechanics lever in my whole payload.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US BLS OES annual median hourly wage for NAICS 62 (health care and social assistance) relative to the all-industry median, plus the share of NAICS 62 employment in the three lowest-paid occupational groups (home health/personal care aides, nursing assistants).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031 (OES reference year 2030)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If the aide share of NAICS 62 employment has risen >= 3 pp while the NAICS 62 median lags the all-industry median, my CQ1 composition artefact is confirmed and CQ1 drops to 8. If the aide share is flat and the NAICS 62 median outpaces by >= 1 pp/yr, CQ1 rises to 18-20.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative OECD-38 + China strict operational data-centre electricity consumption reported by IEA/national regulators, together with FERC/ENTSO-E/CEC interconnection queue clearance times and HV transformer lead times.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If DC consumption is tracking above ~700 TWh by 2031 while interconnection queue times and transformer lead times are FALLING, the weakest-link cap is not binding: CQ3 P50 rises 1.85 -> 2.10, P90 2.65 -> 3.00, CQ6-A 11 -> 17. If DC consumption is tracking above 700 TWh while queue times lengthen and electrician wage growth accelerates, CQ3 P50 is unchanged but CQ6-C rises 28 -> 34 and CQ1's second conjunct gains ~4 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The first MSCI World peak-to-trough drawdown of >= 20% after Jan 1 2027, and the sign and magnitude of the change in G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yields between the equity peak and trough dates; plus the trailing 24-month average MOVE at that point.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"If yields RISE or fall by less than 50 bp through that drawdown, CQ5 rises from 26 to ~45-50 (conjunct 2 largely satisfied, and the episode lifts the MOVE average). If yields fall >= 100 bp in a clean flight-to-quality and trailing MOVE is below 90, CQ5 falls to ~14 and CQ4's P10 drops toward 0.6.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 audit seat, effort 'growth'."}}